TUnit uses `[Before(…)]` and `[After(…)]` attributes with a `HookType` enum to control scope. Here's a sketch for each:

## 1. Before/After Every Test (method-level)

```csharp
public class MyTests
{
    [Before(Test)]
    public void ResetDatabase()
    {
        // Runs before each [Test] in this class
        InMemoryDb.Reset();
    }

    [After(Test)]
    public void Cleanup()
    {
        // Runs after each [Test] in this class
    }

    [Test]
    public void OrderIsCreated() { /* ... */ }
}
```

## 2. Once Per Class (class-level)

```csharp
public class MyTests
{
    [Before(Class)]
    public static void OpenFixture()
    {
        // Once before the first test in this class (must be static)
        SharedFixture = new DatabaseFixture();
    }

    [After(Class)]
    public static void DisposeFixture()
    {
        SharedFixture.Dispose();
    }

    static DatabaseFixture SharedFixture;

    [Test]
    public void Test1() { /* use SharedFixture */ }
}
```

## 3. Once Per Assembly

```csharp
// Can live in any file in the assembly
public class GlobalSetup
{
    [Before(Assembly)]
    public static void StartWireMock()
    {
        WireMockServer = WireMockServer.Start();
    }

    [After(Assembly)]
    public static void StopWireMock()
    {
        WireMockServer.Stop();
    }
}
```

## 4. Once Per Test Session (entire run)

```csharp
public class SessionSetup
{
    [Before(TestSession)]
    public static void ApplyMigrations()
    {
        // Very first thing in the entire test run
        Database.Migrate();
    }

    [After(TestSession)]
    public static void TearDownDatabase()
    {
        Database.Drop();
    }
}
```

## Summary

| Scope | Attribute | Method must be |
|-------|-----------|----------------|
| Each test | `[Before(Test)]` / `[After(Test)]` | Instance or static |
| Class | `[Before(Class)]` / `[After(Class)]` | **Static** |
| Assembly | `[Before(Assembly)]` / `[After(Assembly)]` | **Static** |
| Session | `[Before(TestSession)]` / `[After(TestSession)]` | **Static** |

All hooks support `async Task` return types. The `HookType` values (`Test`, `Class`, `Assembly`, `TestSession`) come from `using static TUnit.Core.HookType;`.
